WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.922 --> 00:00:04.130
DAVID MCCLUSKY: Hey everyone.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.740
My name is David McClusky.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.040
And I'm a solutions engineer
with the Google Maps API team.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.340
So as a solutions
engineer, I work

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.370
with Google's top
customers and partners

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.870
to help them solve
their business

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.190
problems with the power
of the Google Maps API.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.100
So as Monica and Paul
mentioned, location intelligence

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.810
has become
increasingly important

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.180
to businesses of all sizes.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.970
But only now are they really
available to businesses

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.229
of any size.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:36.460
So you don't need to be an Uber
or an Amazon to take advantage

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.350
of location intelligence.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.840
Any startup, any company can
build a platform on Google Maps

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.150
through the Google Maps API.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.400
So I'm going to tell you
a little bit about some

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.430
of the APIs that Paul showed
you on a previous slide,

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.971
how they can be used in
a real life situation.

00:00:53.971 --> 00:00:55.720
So I'm going to show
you a demo today that

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.300
demonstrates how a small company
can use location intelligence.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.810
This is a fictional
coffee delivery

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.370
service called Coffee Pop.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.786
And I'm going to
show you how they

00:01:07.786 --> 00:01:09.940
can use the API to
drive better engagement,

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:16.840
streamline their operations,
and deliver greater satisfaction

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.750
to their customer base.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.120
OK, so Coffee Pop,
what is Coffee Pop?

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.940
So this is a small,
again, fictional coffee

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.560
and cake delivery service.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.330
I stress fictional
because last time

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.621
we had people trying to
download the app on their phone

00:01:30.621 --> 00:01:33.560
and order coffee, and it
wasn't working out for them.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.300
So Coffee Pop, they brew
coffee out of barista trucks.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.160
They have a bicycle
riding workforce

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.520
that delivers coffee and cake
to people at their own location

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.300
on demand.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.383
And then they can move
their barista trucks around

00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:49.540
to better optimize their
service area to maximize

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.387
the number of customers.

00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:53.470
So therefore they need to
connect their customers,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:57.520
riders, and delivery trucks
together in real time

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.450
to keep everyone informed
about current operations

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.980
and the itinerary and
allow management to keep

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.630
an eye on the entire operation.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.910
So to do this, they built
three separate applications,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.680
one for the customers,
one for the riders,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:12.721
and one for the managers.

00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:15.190
So what does the
management team do?

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.740
So they have an app
to view and improve

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.870
the real time performance
of their entire operation

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.760
in real time.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.770
The customers have
an application

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.870
so they can place an
order for coffee and cake

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.220
at their location.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.900
And then the
delivery riders have

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.450
an app so they know when
there is an order ready

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.722
and to get routed
to the customer.

00:02:34.722 --> 00:02:37.180
So I'm going to show you how
each one of these applications

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.358
can be built with
the Google Maps APIs.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.900
All right, so this is
our management dashboard.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.960
So this provides a
view for management

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.270
into the entire operation
of their business.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.000
So we have things here on
this dashboard like customer

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.760
satisfaction scores, the
number of active orders,

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.300
the percent of orders that
are late or behind schedule.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.440
I can see recent feedback.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.400
You know, someone says three
stars, my coffee was on time.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.620
Someone says one star, you
were three minutes late.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:12.560
I can also view a map.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.210
So this map gives me
a live, real time view

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.550
into everything that's going
on in my delivery zone.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.680
So what I have
here in the middle,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.320
this icon here represents
my barista truck.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.480
This is where we're
brewing the coffee.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.330
So I can click on it and I
can view our entire delivery

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.560
service area.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.980
So this polygon
here represents--

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.235
it's what we call an isochron.

00:03:34.235 --> 00:03:38.215
It's everywhere you can drive
within 15 minutes in this case.

00:03:38.215 --> 00:03:42.550
So we want to set up a
delivery zone in real time

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.560
to ensure that all
of our customers

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.420
can be serviced
within 15 minutes.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.220
So we're using real
time traffic data

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.806
to generate this in real time.

00:03:50.806 --> 00:03:52.430
Let's look at some
of these other icons

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.429
and what they represent.

00:03:53.429 --> 00:03:56.890
So here we have
a delivery rider.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.570
I can click and zoom on him.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.490
So this is one of
our bicycle riders.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.780
And I can view what
his current task is.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.080
So he's right now tasked
to go pick up coffee

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.820
at a barista truck
and then deliver it

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.760
to this customer over here.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.050
I can also see these icons.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.720
You see the little happy faces.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.880
And if I zoom, yeah, there's
some frowns over here.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.060
These are customers that
have already been serviced

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.310
but are either happy or unhappy.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.830
So I see over here I have
a couple unhappy customers.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:31.060
So then I want to dig down
and try to investigate, why

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.770
were these customers unhappy?

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.030
Was their orders wrong
or maybe they were late?

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.360
And I can bring in other
layers of information,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.250
other sources of
information to try

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.530
to analyze what went wrong
here and better understand

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:42.654
the situation.

00:04:42.654 --> 00:04:45.070
So one thing I can do is I can
turn on a real time traffic

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.620
layer right here in the app,
and I can view real time traffic

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.470
conditions to see if
maybe that's impacting

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:51.730
our service in this area.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:54.445
Maybe I need to change our
service area a little bit.

00:04:57.902 --> 00:04:59.360
So the next thing
I'm going to show

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.480
is now what the
Coffee Pop experience

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.940
looks like from the
perspective of a customer

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:05.820
from a mobile app.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.000
So here I have an
iOS application.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.084
This is built with the
Google Maps SDK for iOS.

00:05:13.084 --> 00:05:14.750
And in this case what
the customer would

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.970
be doing and interacting
with this experience

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:23.030
is dragging the pin around
here to indicate where they

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.652
want their coffee delivered to.

00:05:24.652 --> 00:05:27.110
Typically this is just going
to be their existing location.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.496
So we get their location
from the device and say,

00:05:29.496 --> 00:05:31.370
this is where I want
the coffee delivered to.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.002
But perhaps you're on
your way to the park

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:36.710
and you want your
coffee delivered there.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.200
So you can move the map pin.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.660
Now the interesting thing here
is the number you see here

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.350
at the bottom of the screen.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:44.870
So this is the time estimate.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:48.140
Now a lot of things are going
into this time estimate.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.630
It's the amount
of time that it's

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:53.120
going to take the delivery
rider to get to the barista

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.920
truck, the amount
of time it's going

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.710
to take to process the order and
then get that order over here

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:58.460
to the customer.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.700
And so it's doing an
assignment from the orders

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.660
to the delivery riders in
real time in an automated

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:06.830
fashion behind the scenes.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:08.480
So there's no dispatcher
here involved.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.600
This is all just happening
in an automated fashion.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.100
So now that I know
my time estimate,

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.350
I can click to place my order.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.990
I can say I want some
coffee, some cake.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.840
I click Confirm.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.820
It's been assigned
to a delivery rider.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.000
I can see a map.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.060
I get time estimate.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.190
And I can click Order Now.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.480
And then now I can monitor, in
real time, that delivery driver

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.640
as they come to
deliver me my coffee.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.113
So I can see what's
going on here.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.750
And then I will now show
you the third application.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.160
So this is the application
that the delivery riders

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.494
would have with them
on their mobile phone.

00:06:53.494 --> 00:06:55.660
So in this case this is an
Android-based application

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.162
using our Android API.

00:06:58.162 --> 00:06:59.620
So the first thing
that it would do

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.740
is detect that the
delivery rider is inside

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.980
one of the active barista
truck delivery zones.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.030
And once it is, once
that rider is there,

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:11.410
they are allowed to sign
into the application.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.290
And instantly they're
matched up with a job

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.950
that's waiting for them.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:19.030
So they can accept the job and
they can view the active job.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.560
And in this case,
it doesn't really

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:21.976
actually the most
sense just to be

00:07:21.976 --> 00:07:23.610
assigned to one delivery job.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:24.610
It could be several.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:27.687
So you want to go to a barista
truck, pick up several orders,

00:07:27.687 --> 00:07:28.770
and then deliver them all.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:32.386
So our Directions APIs being
used here behind the scenes.

00:07:32.386 --> 00:07:33.760
And what that's
doing is actually

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.290
doing waypoint optimization.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.290
So it's routing them in
the most efficient route

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.720
to these multiple destinations.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.856
They can view the-- first
thing I need to select a job,

00:07:45.856 --> 00:07:46.480
activate a job.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:46.979
Yes?

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:49.889
AUDIENCE: Is it doing the
traveling salesman algorithm?

00:07:49.889 --> 00:07:52.180
DAVID MCCLUSKY: It's doing
a variation of the traveling

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.020
salesman problem.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:59.650
So in this case it's one
worker and up to-- we

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.020
can do up to several
dozen waypoints in this.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:08.140
It's not doing
hundreds or thousands

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.900
of waypoints, which becomes
very computationally intensive.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.740
But with a smaller subset,
yeah, we can do that.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:20.860
So the driver's given
a route, and he's

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.380
given the set of
directions to go to and do

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.395
those multiple deliveries.

00:08:25.395 --> 00:08:26.770
And when the
deliveries are done,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.824
you can mark them as complete.

00:08:29.824 --> 00:08:31.240
Or what you can
do is actually use

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:32.870
the location from
the device itself.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.411
So if he's actually
traveling around,

00:08:34.411 --> 00:08:36.730
it could detect that
the driver has entered

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.140
into the same zone as the
customer location and said,

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.440
hey, that delivery's been made.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.310
Mark those jobs as complete.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.640
So this is just
a few of the ways

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:51.150
you can take advantage
of the Google Maps APIs

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:54.820
to build out an entire business
in an automated fashion.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.660
So it doesn't require
much infrastructure.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.785
It doesn't require servers.

00:08:57.785 --> 00:08:59.250
It doesn't require data centers.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.230
You can build this all
in the cloud with just

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:05.880
a few applications,
and really power

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.960
your business with location.

